## Why this is a pointer

State the reason in the file, so the next person to add a paragraph sees it:

> An always-on file that duplicates gated content will drift, and the drift is invisible — both
> copies read as authoritative and nothing compares them. Two rules on <this project> already
> rotted that way. Keeping the substance behind a gate is not tidiness; it is the only version of
> it that anything can check.

And then check it, because a convention nobody enforces is a convention that decays:

```
python check_pointer_file.py <path/to/CLAUDE.md> --rules-dir <path/to/rules>
```

That asserts the three properties a script can hold — every pointer resolves, no line here
duplicates a gated rule, and the file is inside the budget you set. It cannot tell you whether a
rule belongs here. Nothing can; that judgement is the one you have to keep making.
